ouadi Maakoul and Deepseek and chatGpt and Gemini 

Epistemic status. This document is a theoretical and methodological program, not an experimental report. No empirical result is presented as established. Three sources of truth are strictly distinguished: standard results from the literature; own constructions, labeled as such; and conditional conjectures, confined to the final chapter and closed by default. The thesis body is self-contained: no load-bearing claim depends on material outside it.



1. Introduction

Three research programs have reached independent maturity.

Stochastic thermodynamics of computation. Landauer (1961) establishes that irreversible erasure of one bit dissipates at minimum k_B T \ln 2. Jarzynski (1997) and Crooks (1999) give exact non-equilibrium equalities constraining work fluctuations. Tkachenko (2025) derives thermodynamic bounds for deep neural network inference, demonstrating that linear operations can in principle be performed reversibly whereas nonlinear activation functions impose an unavoidable energy cost determined by the average number of neurons undergoing state transition per inference. The fundamental thermodynamic bound is restated in terms of negentropy, a metric more universal than energy for assessing thermodynamic cost of information processing. These results motivate, but do not establish, a universal relation between measured material energy and entropy production.

Information geometry. Rao (1945) and Amari (1985) endow the manifold of statistical distributions with a Fisher–Rao metric and \alpha-affine connections. For suitable equilibrium statistical-mechanical families, the Ruppeiner metric can be related to the Fisher information metric, with the correspondence depending on thermodynamic representation and assumptions. This equivalence is rigorous for equilibrium systems, not contemporary digital substrates. MCD uses only that KL divergence is a well-defined functional of distributions.

Causal inference under intervention. Pearl (2000) defines the \mathit{do}(\cdot) operator. Peters, Bühlmann, and Meinshausen (2016) formalize Invariant Causal Prediction, exploiting invariance of causal prediction across experimental settings. ICP is one framework, not a general definition of external validity.

Within the literature reviewed here, no protocol jointly evaluates computational allocation, measured material energy, and interventional predictive adequacy under matched information and action constraints. Material energy is measured as a property of computational execution and is not, by construction, defined as a function of the adequacy of the output.

The guiding question is a decision problem under constraint:

\pi^\star(E_{\max})
=
\arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi}
\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}(\pi)]
\quad
\text{s.t.}
\quad
\mathbb{E}[E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi)] \leq E_{\max},
\tag{1.1}

where \mathcal{I} is an interventional predictive adequacy score and E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}} is measured material energy within a declared hardware accounting boundary. The empirical question is:

Does a computational policy that uses an estimate of interventional predictive gain as a decision signal produce non-dominated operating points on the (\mathcal{I}, E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}) front that a metareasoning policy with identical information, identical action space, and identical energy signal does not?

This tests

\boxed{
\text{interventional value signal}
\;\rightarrow\;
\text{better computational allocation}
}

under a measured energy constraint.

The contributions are: a decision-theoretic framework with explicit Pareto set definition and a conditional proposition relating multivariate stochastic dominance to Pareto expansion under a stated compatibility assumption; operational definitions including strictly proper utility targets, three diagnostic oracles, uncertainty-adjusted optimistic value estimation, independent \lambda calibration per policy, and a three-level leakage audit; a pre-registrable protocol with split hypotheses (H_{1a} Pareto expansion, H_{1b} center-outward q-dominance), simulation-based power analysis, Bayesian ICC estimation, and manifest locking; and a conditional conjecture on E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}} \to \Sigma_{\text{prod}}, closed by default.

