2.2. Primal Problem and Pareto Set

Definition 2.1 (Operating point set). For a policy space \Pi and a budget grid \mathcal{E} = \{E_1, \ldots, E_J\},

\mathcal{P}
=
\left\{
(E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi),\mathcal{I}(\pi))
:
\pi\in\Pi
\right\}.
\tag{2.1}

Definition 2.2 (Pareto frontier). The Pareto frontier is

\mathcal{F}
=
\operatorname{Pareto}(\mathcal{P})
=
\left\{
p \in \mathcal{P}
:
\nexists\, q \in \mathcal{P}
\text{ s.t. }
q \prec p
\right\},
\tag{2.2}

where q \prec p denotes weak dominance: E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(q) \leq E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(p) and \mathcal{I}(q) \geq \mathcal{I}(p), with at least one strict inequality.

Definition 2.3 (Downward closure). The downward closure of a frontier \mathcal{F} is

\operatorname{Down}(\mathcal{F})
=
\left\{
(E, \mathcal{I})
:
\exists\, (E', \mathcal{I}') \in \mathcal{F}
\text{ s.t. }
E \geq E' \text{ and } \mathcal{I} \leq \mathcal{I}'
\right\}.
\tag{2.3}

The constrained optimum at budget E_{\max} is

\pi^\star(E_{\max})
=
\arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi}
\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}(\pi)]
\quad
\text{s.t.}
\quad
\mathbb{E}[E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi)] \leq E_{\max}.
\tag{2.4}

\pi^\star(E_{\max}) is a constrained optimum at a particular budget. \mathcal{F} is the set of non-dominated operating points across budgets.

2.3. Dominance-to-Frontier Compatibility

The relationship between multivariate stochastic dominance and Pareto expansion is not unconditional. It depends on a compatibility assumption connecting the stochastic order to the attainable operating-point sets.

Assumption 2.4 (Dominance-to-frontier compatibility). Let P_{\mathrm{MCD}} and P_{\mathrm{base}} be probability distributions on the outcome space \mathcal{Z} = \mathbb{R}^2 equipped with the orientation Z = (-E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}, \mathcal{I}). Let \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} be a specified multivariate stochastic order. There exists a measurable mapping \Phi: \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) \to 2^{\mathbb{R}^2} assigning to each outcome distribution an attainable operating-point set, such that

P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} P_{\mathrm{base}}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\Phi(P_{\mathrm{MCD}}) \not\subseteq \operatorname{Down}(\Phi(P_{\mathrm{base}})).
\tag{2.5}

Proposition 2.5. Under Assumption 2.4, if P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} P_{\mathrm{base}}, then the induced attainable set of MCD is not contained in the downward closure of the baseline frontier.

Proof. Immediate from Assumption 2.4. \square

Corollary 2.6. H_{1b} (multivariate stochastic dominance) is a sufficient condition for H_{1a} (Pareto expansion) only under the compatibility assumption. The converse does not hold: a policy can produce a new non-dominated operating point without its entire outcome distribution dominating the baseline's.

The scientific content resides in Assumption 2.4, which must be established separately for the chosen stochastic order. The question of when distributional dominance induces expansion of the attainable frontier remains an open mathematical problem and is itself a legitimate research question.

2.4. Center-Outward Distribution and Quantile Functions

The protocol adopts the optimal-transport-based center-outward functions of Hallin et al. (2021), which push forward the uniform distribution on the unit ball to the target distribution and vice versa.

Definition 2.7 (Center-outward quantile function). For a distribution P on \mathbb{R}^d with finite first moment, the center-outward quantile function is the optimal transport map from the uniform distribution U_d on the unit ball \mathbb{B}_d to P:

Q_P^{\pm}: \mathbb{B}_d \to \mathbb{R}^d,
\qquad
Q_P^{\pm}{}_{\#}U_d = P.
\tag{2.6}

Definition 2.8 (Center-outward distribution function). The center-outward distribution function is the inverse transport map:

F_P^{\pm}: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{B}_d,
\qquad
F_P^{\pm}{}_{\#}P = U_d.
\tag{2.7}

The norm \|F_P^{\pm}(z)\| provides the center-outward rank of z, and the direction F_P^{\pm}(z)/\|F_P^{\pm}(z)\| provides the sign. The functions F_P^{\pm} and Q_P^{\pm} are homeomorphisms under regularity conditions on P.

2.5. Center-Outward q-Dominance

Definition 2.9 (q-dominance). For two distributions P and Q on \mathbb{R}^d, P q-dominates Q, written P \succeq_q Q, if for all y \in \mathbb{R}^d,

Q_P^{\pm}(F_Q^{\pm}(y)) \geq y,
\tag{2.8}

where the inequality is understood componentwise in the oriented coordinate system Z = (-E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}, \mathcal{I}).

Theorem 2.10 (van der Laag et al., 2026). If P \succeq_q Q for all quantile levels q \in [0,1), then P strongly first-order stochastically dominates Q. The q-dominance relation is a sample-computable proxy for strong FSD in stochastic multi-objective optimization.

The protocol inherits this result by reference. The theoretical guarantee is not asserted independently.

2.6. Orientation Transformation

The orientation

Z = (-E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}, \mathcal{I})
\tag{2.9}

makes both coordinates "higher is better." Without this transformation, the center-outward quantile function does not respect the desired order. The transformation is pre-registered and applied before any dominance testing.

2.7. Local Policy and Optimistic Value Adjustment

At each step t, the agent chooses a \in \mathcal{A}_t according to

a_t^\star
=
\arg\max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_t}
\left[
\widehat{V}_t(a) - \lambda_\pi \widehat{E}_t(a)
\right].
\tag{2.10}

The stopping rule uses an uncertainty-adjusted optimistic value:

\widetilde{V}_t(a) = \widehat{V}_t(a) + \beta_t s_t(a),
\qquad
\max_a \left[\widetilde{V}_t(a) - \lambda_\pi \widehat{E}_t(a)\right] \leq 0
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{terminate},
\tag{2.11}

where s_t(a) is an uncertainty estimate and \beta_t is a pre-registered optimism coefficient calibrated empirically through validation. No confidence guarantee is claimed for \widetilde{V}_t: it is an optimistic value adjustment, not a statistically valid upper confidence bound.

Proposition 2.11 (Stochastic estimation error creates premature termination risk). Let \widehat{V}_t be any estimator of V_t with \mathrm{Var}(\widehat{V}_t) > 0. The naive stopping rule

\max_a \left[\widehat{V}_t(a) - \lambda_\pi \widehat{E}_t(a)\right] \leq 0
\;\Rightarrow\;
\text{terminate}
\tag{2.12}

terminates with positive probability on steps where the true optimal continuation value is strictly positive.

Proof. Let a^\star = \arg\max_a [V_t(a) - \lambda_\pi E_t(a)] with V_t(a^\star) - \lambda_\pi E_t(a^\star) = \delta > 0. Since \widehat{V}_t(a^\star) has positive variance, \mathbb{P}[\widehat{V}_t(a^\star) - \lambda_\pi E_t(a^\star) \leq 0] = \mathbb{P}[\widehat{V}_t(a^\star) \leq \lambda_\pi E_t(a^\star)] > 0 whenever \delta < \infty. \square

Remark 2.12. No unbiasedness assumption is required. The result holds under any estimator with positive variance. In the real system, \widehat{V}_t will be learned, biased, dependent on h_t, and correlated with previous observations. The proposition establishes a qualitative risk, not a quantitative bound.

2.8. Duality and Marginal Price

\mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda)
=
\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}(\pi)]
-
\lambda \left(\mathbb{E}[E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi)] - E_{\max}\right).
\tag{2.13}

Definition 2.13 (Penalty parameter). \lambda is an energy-performance penalty parameter. It is not a dual optimum in the convex-analysis sense.

Proposition 2.14 (Local marginal price interpretation). When the empirical frontier \mathcal{F} is locally Lipschitz and admits a supporting hyperplane, the variation of the optimal value with the budget can be interpreted locally as

\frac{\partial}{\partial E_{\max}}
\max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{I}(\pi)]
\;\approx\;
\lambda^\star(E_{\max}).
\tag{2.14}

This is a local, empirical interpretation. It is not a global optimality condition.

Proposition 2.15 (Non-convexity of the primal). The policy space \Pi is in general non-convex. The dual function g(\lambda) = \sup_\pi \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) may not be differentiable, and primal recovery from the dual solution is not guaranteed.

Proof. The set of deterministic policies over a finite action space is a finite set, hence non-convex. Learned value functions introduce non-convexity through composition with neural networks. Primal recovery requires strong duality, which fails without convexity. \square

