3. Operational Definitions

3.1. Interventional Predictive Adequacy Score

Definition 3.1 (Post-hoc interventional ground truth).

\mathcal{I}^\star(\theta)
=
-\sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \,
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(
P_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)
\,\Big\|\,
q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)
\right).
\tag{3.1}

Weights w_e = 1/E, uniform over test environments, pre-registered.

Definition 3.2 (Online estimation).

\widehat{\mathcal{I}}_t(\theta_{\leq t})
=
-\sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \,
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\left(
\hat{P}_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)
\,\Big\|\,
q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X; \theta_{\leq t})
\right),
\tag{3.2}

with \hat{P}_e^{\mathit{do}} estimated on a calibration set disjoint from training and test.

Definition 3.3 (Interventional interface). The model receives (x, m, x_{\mathit{do}}). The interventional distribution is defined by construction:

q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid \mathit{do}(X_j = x_j))
\equiv
q_\theta(Y \mid \tilde{x}, m = e_j, x_{\mathit{do}} = x_j e_j).
\tag{3.3}

Semantic disclaimer. The protocol does not evaluate the model's ability to discover an unknown causal structure. It evaluates predictive accuracy under interventions whose semantics are explicitly provided by the interface. The causality of the score comes from the interventional ground truth generated by the SCM, not from the mere presence of an intervention token in the model input. The metric is therefore named interventional predictive adequacy, not causal score. The distinction

\boxed{
\text{interventional semantics supplied}
\neq
\text{causal model identified}
}
\tag{3.4}

is preserved throughout.

3.2. Material Energy

Definition 3.4 (Hardware accounting boundary).

\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}
=
\{\text{GPU, CPU, memory, accelerator, PSU, etc. explicitly included}\}.
\tag{3.5}

Definition 3.5 (Measured energy). The measured energy within the accounting boundary over a measurement window [\tau_0, \tau_1] is

E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi)
=
\int_{\tau_0}^{\tau_1}
P_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(t) \, dt,
\tag{3.6}

where

P_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(t)
=
P_{\mathrm{GPU}}(t)
+
P_{\mathrm{CPU}}(t)
+
P_{\mathrm{memory}}(t)
+
P_{\mathrm{PSU}}(t)
+
\cdots
\tag{3.7}

sums over the components explicitly included in the boundary. The decomposition

E_{\text{inf}} + E_{\text{sample}} + E_{\text{verif}} + E_{\text{select}} + E_{\text{policy}}

is an accounting decomposition of E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}, not a physical definition. Double counting is avoided by pre-registering the component list and the measurement interfaces: NVML for GPU power, RAPL for CPU and DRAM power, IPMI for system-level power where available. Components not exposed by hardware counters are excluded from the boundary and reported separately.

Proposition 3.6 (Accounting versus thermodynamic quantities).

E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}
\neq
\Sigma_{\text{prod}},
\qquad
E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}
\neq
E_{\text{total thermodynamic}}.
\tag{3.8}

Proof. \mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}} is an instrument-defined set of components. \Sigma_{\text{prod}} is entropy production, a thermodynamic quantity of dimension energy/temperature. E_{\text{total thermodynamic}} includes energy dissipated outside the boundary. The two are dimensionally and conceptually distinct. \square

3.3. Action Space

\pi_{\text{atom}} = (k, \ell, v),
\tag{3.9}

where k is the candidate index, \ell the effective depth, and v \in \{0, 1\} the verification indicator. Cost:

E_{\text{atom}}(k, \ell, v)
=
E_{\text{layer}} \cdot \ell + v \cdot E_{\text{verif}}.
\tag{3.10}

Execution semantics. Pre-registered rules specify: how \ell maps to actual forward passes through the model; how v triggers verification and what the verification function computes; how candidate k is selected; and the exact computation graph for each action sequence.

3.4. History and Leakage Audit

h_t
=
\left(
x, \{(k_i, \ell_i, v_i)\}_{i<t}, \{\widehat{E}_i\}_{i<t}, \{\widehat{\mathcal{I}}_i\}_{i<t}, \theta_{\leq t}
\right).
\tag{3.11}

Gate 1 — Static provenance audit. Automatically verify that

h_t \cap
\{\text{test data}, \text{test labels}, \mathcal{I}^\star, \text{future action outcomes}\}
=
\varnothing.
\tag{3.12}

Gate 2 — Canary audit. Inject synthetic secret variables into forbidden channels. If the policy responds to the canary, leakage exists.

Gate 3 — Predictive probe diagnostic. Multiple probes of increasing capacity \mathcal{G}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{G}_K (e.g., linear, gradient-boosted trees, small MLP) predict \mathcal{I}^\star from h_t. A pre-registered diagnostic threshold is calibrated on a pilot run with known absence of leakage. Leakage is flagged if any probe exceeds the threshold. Failure to detect leakage is a diagnostic, not a proof of absence: no finite probe can certify the absence of leakage, because the set of measurable functions is uncountable.

