Definition 3.6 (Logarithmic score).

\widehat{U}_t(\theta_{\leq t})
=
\sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \,
\mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}(X, Y)}
\!\left[
\log q_\theta(Y \mid X; \theta_{\leq t})
\right].
\tag{3.13}

Proposition 3.7 (Log loss is strictly proper). The logarithmic score is uniquely maximized at q = P among all distributions q.

Proof. S(q, P) - S(P, P) = \mathbb{E}_P[\log(q/P)] = -\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| q) \leq 0, with equality iff q = P almost everywhere. \square

Corollary 3.8. B4_{\text{Pred}} must use log loss (or another strictly proper scoring rule), not 0–1 loss, to avoid degenerate optima.

3.6. Equivalence Under Matched Information

Proposition 3.9. If B4_{\text{Pred}} uses the same interventional model q_\theta^{\mathit{do}} as MCD, then

\mathcal{I}(\theta)
=
U(\theta)
-
\sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \, H(P_e^{\mathit{do}}),
\tag{3.14}

where H(P_e^{\mathit{do}}) is the entropy of the true interventional distribution in environment e, independent of \theta, t, and action.

Proof. The KL divergence decomposes as

\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(P_e^{\mathit{do}} \| q^{\mathit{do}})
=
\mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}[\log P_e^{\mathit{do}}]
-
\mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}[\log q^{\mathit{do}}].
\tag{3.15}

Summing over e with weights w_e, the first term is the weighted negative entropy, independent of q^{\mathit{do}}. The second term is U(\theta). Therefore \mathcal{I}(\theta) = U(\theta) - \sum_e w_e H(P_e^{\mathit{do}}). Since the entropy term is constant across actions and policies, the marginal gains \Delta \mathcal{I} and \Delta U are identical. \square

Corollary 3.10. Under matched information with log loss, the MCD policy and B4_{\text{Pred}} optimize mathematically equivalent objectives. The comparison tests nothing beyond implementation noise.

3.7. Reframed Research Questions

The algebraic equivalence under matched information motivates a separation of research questions.

RQ1 (Primary): Does access to explicit interventional semantics improve energy-constrained computational allocation relative to observational predictive allocation?

H_2^{\mathrm{VoCI}}: \Delta \mathcal{I}(E_3) > 0 \text{ vs } B4_{\text{Pred}} \text{ (observational)}.
\tag{3.16}

RQ2 (Secondary): Conditional on matched interventional information, does the metareasoning policy differ from alternative allocation policies?

H_2^{\mathrm{matched}}: \Delta \mathcal{I}(E_3) > 0 \text{ vs } B4_{\text{Pred}} \text{ (matched interface, alternative scoring rule)}.
\tag{3.17}

This separation prevents the conflation of the value of causal information with the value of causal metareasoning.

3.8. Option B as Scoring-Rule Robustness Analysis

The Brier score comparison is not a simple diagnostic. The log score corresponds to KL divergence; the Brier score corresponds to quadratic divergence. A difference in performance between MCD (KL) and B4_{\text{Pred}} (Brier) would confound the metareasoning policy with the geometry of the scoring function. The correct framing is: conditional on matched interventional information, does the choice of strictly proper scoring rule affect the Pareto frontier? This is a legitimate secondary question, presented as scoring-rule robustness analysis rather than independent confirmation.

3.9. Three Oracles

Definition 3.11 (Matched-horizon interventional oracle).

\widehat{V}^{\text{Oracle}}_{t, H=1}(a)
=
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{I}^\star_{t+1} - \mathcal{I}^\star_t \mid h_t, a\right].
\tag{3.18}

Definition 3.12 (Full-horizon interventional oracle).

\widehat{V}^{\text{Oracle}}_{t, H=\infty}(a)
=
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\mathcal{I}^\star_{\text{final}} - \mathcal{I}^\star_t \mid h_t, a\right].
\tag{3.19}

Definition 3.13 (Predictive utility oracle).

\widehat{V}^{\text{Oracle-U}}_{t, H=1}(a)
=
\mathbb{E}\!\left[U^\star_{t+1} - U^\star_t \mid h_t, a\right].
\tag{3.20}

Proposition 3.14 (Diagnostic decomposition). Under the assumption that estimation error and planning error are additive in the value scale,

\underbrace{\text{MCD-Learned} \to \text{MCD-Oracle}_{H=1}}_{\text{estimation}}
+
\underbrace{\text{MCD-Oracle}_{H=1} \to \text{MCD-Oracle}_{H=\infty}}_{\text{planning}}
+
\underbrace{\text{MCD-Oracle}_{H=1} \leftrightarrow \text{B4}_{\text{Pred}}\text{-Oracle}_{H=1}}_{\text{signal content}}
\tag{3.21}

approximates the total observed gap. This is an operational decomposition, not necessarily an additive identity.

Proof. The decomposition is a definitional accounting identity under the additivity assumption. If the value function is non-additive, the decomposition is an approximation whose error is bounded by the interaction term. \square

