4.4. Formalization of H_{1a} as a Population-Level Claim

H1a (Population). Let \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}^{\mathrm{pop}} and \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}^{\mathrm{pop}} be the population-level Pareto frontiers. Then

\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}^{\mathrm{pop}} \not\subseteq \operatorname{Down}(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}^{\mathrm{pop}}).
\tag{4.2}

The empirical test requires a well-defined functional measuring the violation. Define the directional violation functional

\Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}})
=
\sup_{p \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}}
\inf_{q \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}}
\max
\left[
\frac{E_q - E_p - \tau_E}{s_E},
\frac{\mathcal{I}_p - \mathcal{I}_q - \tau_{\mathcal{I}}}{s_{\mathcal{I}}}
\right],
\tag{4.3}

where \tau_E and \tau_{\mathcal{I}} are pre-registered tolerances, and s_E and s_{\mathcal{I}} are scale parameters. The null hypothesis is

H_{1a,0}: \Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}^{\mathrm{pop}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}^{\mathrm{pop}}) \leq 0.
\tag{4.4}

Rejection requires the empirical violation functional to exceed zero with statistical significance under bootstrap resampling of the experimental units. This transforms H1a from a methodological phrase into a testable functional.

4.5. Formalization of H_{1b} via Center-Outward q-Dominance

The test is:

H_0: P_{\mathrm{base}} \succeq_q P_{\mathrm{MCD}},
\tag{4.5}

H_1: P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succeq_q P_{\mathrm{base}}.
\tag{4.6}

Incomplete outcome. The complement of H_0 is not simply H_1. There are three possibilities:

P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succ_q P_{\mathrm{base}},
\qquad
P_{\mathrm{base}} \succ_q P_{\mathrm{MCD}},
\qquad
\text{Incomparable}.
\tag{4.7}

The third case is precisely the incomparable outcome that matters for a partial order. The test should recognize explicitly: MCD dominates / Baseline dominates / Incomparable, rather than treating the problem as a simple binary test.

Empirical procedure. The protocol adopts the empirical test procedure of van der Laag et al. (2026), which uses the center-outward grids, ranks, and signs to verify the inequality (2.8) componentwise. The test statistic is computed using the sample-computable procedure described therein. Inference uses the explicit sample-size threshold n^\star(\delta) derived from the center-outward transformation to control Type I error at level \delta.

Independent robustness analysis. The entropy-regularized optimal transport approach of Ma, Liu, and Zhuang (2025) provides an independent framework with different MSD concepts, consistent Kolmogorov–Smirnov and Cramér–von Mises test statistics, and established bootstrap validity. This is used as a robustness analysis, not as the primary inference procedure.

Primary framework selection. van der Laag et al. q-dominance is the primary framework for H_{1b}. Ma et al. is an independent robustness analysis. The definitions, interpretations, and bootstrap procedures of the two papers are not interchangeable and are not mixed.

4.6. Gatekeeping Control for Nested Hypotheses

A gatekeeping procedure controls the family-wise error rate when hypotheses are tested in a fixed order. The procedure is:

1. Test H_{1a} at level \alpha_1.
2. If H_{1a} is rejected, proceed to test H_{1b} at level \alpha_2.
3. The FWER is controlled at \alpha_1 + \alpha_2 under the global null.

Alternatively, a hierarchical closed-testing procedure controls the FWER by testing hypotheses in a fixed order and stopping at the first non-rejection. The protocol pre-registers:

· The order of testing (H_{1a} then H_{1b}).
· The levels \alpha_1 and \alpha_2.
· The FWER control method (Bonferroni gatekeeping or closed testing).

4.7. Cross-Family Coefficient of Variation

\mathrm{CV}_j
=
\frac{
\operatorname{SD}_{i \in \{1, 2, 3\}}\!\left[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)\right]
}{
\operatorname{Mean}_{i \in \{1, 2, 3\}}\!\left[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)\right]
},
\qquad
j = 1, \dots, 5.
\tag{4.8}

Criterion: \max_j \mathrm{CV}_j \leq 0.20, with auxiliary condition \mathrm{Mean}_i[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)] > \epsilon_\lambda = 10^{-3}. This measures cross-family variation only. It does not measure intra-family stability, between-seed stability, between-budget stability, or optimizer stability.

4.8. Dual Ascent Convergence Diagnostic

\Delta_{\text{dual}}(F_i, E_j)
=
\left|
\mathbb{E}[E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi^\star(\lambda^\star_{k_{\max}}))]
-
E_{\max}
\right|.
\tag{4.9}

Threshold: \Delta_{\text{dual}} > \eta \cdot \sigma_{E}, where \sigma_{E} is the documented NVML/RAPL sampling noise from the pilot, and \eta = 3. If \Delta_{\text{dual}} > \eta \cdot \sigma_{E} on any (F_i, E_j), the corresponding \lambda^\star is flagged as non-converged and excluded from the CV computation.

4.9. Paired Hierarchical Model

Since MCD and baseline are executed on the same SCM instances and datasets, the observations are paired. The model uses the paired difference:

\Delta_{s,d,m,i}
=
Y_{s,d,m,i}^{\mathrm{MCD}}
-
Y_{s,d,m,i}^{\mathrm{base}},
\tag{4.10}

with the hierarchical structure

\Delta_{s,d,m,i}
=
\mu + u_s + v_{s,d} + w_{s,d,m} + \varepsilon_{s,d,m,i}.
\tag{4.11}

Indices: s = SCM family/instance, d = dataset realization, m = model/training seed, i = inference/policy seed. REML estimation. One-sided test on \mu, Holm correction across 3 families. The variance components refer to the difference distribution, not the individual outcomes.

4.10. Global Rejection Criterion

The program separates three levels of evidence:

Primary falsification. H_{1a} fails across pre-registered families.

Secondary evidence. H_2 fails.

Strong distributional claim. H_{1b} fails.

The global rejection criterion is: MCD is rejected within the defined protocol if, on at least two of three families, H_{1a} fails. No post-hoc loosening of exclusion criteria. No post-hoc selection of the winning budget. No modification of hypotheses after observation. H_2 and H_{1b} are reported separately and not combined into a composite p-value.

4.11. Pre-Registered Pilot

A pilot is run on T1 alone, pre-registered separately and non-inferential. Its sole outputs are:

1. \tilde{\rho}_u, \tilde{\rho}_v, \tilde{\rho}_w: Bayesian posterior medians of variance components, with 95% credible intervals. Bayesian estimation provides credible intervals that quantify the precision with which variance components and ICCs are estimated.
2. \varepsilon_{\text{interface}}, the observed KL gap between q_\theta^{\mathit{do}} and P_e^{\mathit{do}} on T1.
3. The wall-clock and energy cost of one primitive (k, \ell, v).
4. \sigma_{E}, the documented NVML/RAPL sampling noise.

The pilot uses N_{\text{SCM}}^{\text{pilot}} = 25 instances per topology. Bayesian ICC estimation with weakly informative prior \rho_u \sim \text{Beta}(1.5, 3). The decision rules for N_{\text{SCM}} are justified by simulation: for each candidate \rho_u, the complete experiment is simulated to estimate power, and the smallest N achieving 0.80 power is selected. The mapping from \rho_u to N is pre-registered in the pilot protocol, not chosen arbitrarily.

4.12. Simulation-Based Power Analysis

For candidate N_{\text{SCM}} \in \{20, 30, 45, \ldots\}, simulate the complete experiment:

\text{SCM}
\rightarrow
\text{datasets}
\rightarrow
\text{model seeds}
\rightarrow
\text{inference seeds}
\rightarrow
(\mathcal{I},E)
\rightarrow
\text{frontier}
\rightarrow
H_{1a}.
\tag{4.12}

Estimate

\widehat{\mathrm{Power}}(N)
=
\frac{
\#\{\text{simulated experiments satisfying } H_{1a}\}
}{
R
}.
\tag{4.13}

Choose the smallest N giving at least 0.80 power under a preregistered effect-size scenario. H_{1b} power is estimated conditionally on H_{1a} being satisfied. The analytic sensitivity calculation is retained as a secondary check.

4.13. Manifest Locking

Pre-registration uses an open manifest format that records the metric, comparator, threshold, dataset identifier, and seed for a claim, canonicalised and hashed with SHA-256 to produce a fixed, tamper-evident reference. After the evaluation runs, the observed result is checked against the locked manifest, returning PASS, FAIL, or TAMPERED if criteria were altered after locking. The manifest is locked before any data collection.

