Appendix A. Mathematical Foundations

A.1. Notation and Basic Definitions

Throughout, we work in a measure-theoretic setting. Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}) be a probability space. For a random variable Z: \Omega \to \mathbb{R}^d, we write P_Z for its law. The closed unit ball in \mathbb{R}^d is \mathbb{B}_d = \{u \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|u\| \leq 1\}, and U_d denotes the uniform probability distribution on \mathbb{B}_d, normalized so that U_d(\mathbb{B}_d) = 1. The unit sphere is S^{d-1} = \{u \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|u\| = 1\}.

Definition A.1 (Pareto dominance). For p = (E_p, \mathcal{I}_p) and q = (E_q, \mathcal{I}_q) in \mathbb{R}^2, p weakly dominates q, written p \preceq q, if

E_p \leq E_q \quad \text{and} \quad \mathcal{I}_p \geq \mathcal{I}_q.
\tag{A.1}

p strictly dominates q, written p \prec q, if at least one inequality is strict.

Definition A.2 (Pareto frontier). For \mathcal{P} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2,

\operatorname{Pareto}(\mathcal{P}) = \bigl\{ p \in \mathcal{P} : \nexists\, q \in \mathcal{P} \text{ with } q \prec p \bigr\}.
\tag{A.2}

Definition A.3 (Downward closure). For \mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^2,

\operatorname{Down}(\mathcal{F}) = \bigl\{ (E, \mathcal{I}) : \exists\, (E', \mathcal{I}') \in \mathcal{F} \text{ with } E \geq E' \text{ and } \mathcal{I} \leq \mathcal{I}' \bigr\}.
\tag{A.3}

Definition A.4 (Orientation transformation). The oriented coordinate system is

Z = (-E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}, \mathcal{I}),
\tag{A.4}

so that both coordinates are oriented "higher is better." All dominance, quantile, and frontier computations are performed in the Z-coordinate system.

A.2. Policy-to-Law Operator and Dominance-to-Frontier Compatibility

The mapping from policies to outcome distributions requires an explicit operator, otherwise the definition of the attainable set is circular.

Definition A.5 (Policy-to-law operator). Let \Pi be a policy space and \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) the space of probability distributions on the outcome space \mathcal{Z} = \mathbb{R}^2. The policy-to-law operator

\mathcal{L}: \Pi \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z})
\tag{A.5}

assigns to each policy \pi its declared outcome law \mathcal{L}(\pi) \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}) under the pre-registered generative model.

Definition A.6 (Attainable operating-point set). For a specified outcome law P \in \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{Z}), the attainable operating-point set is

\Phi(P)
=
\bigl\{
(E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi), \mathcal{I}(\pi))
:
\pi \in \Pi, \; \mathcal{L}(\pi) = P
\bigr\}.
\tag{A.6}

The map \Phi is set-valued: two distinct policies may induce the same outcome law but produce different attainable operating points. This set-valuedness is not a defect; it is a feature of the policy space.

Assumption A.7 (Dominance-to-frontier compatibility). Let P_{\mathrm{MCD}} and P_{\mathrm{base}} be probability distributions on \mathcal{Z} equipped with the orientation (A.4). Let \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} be a specified multivariate stochastic order. The policy-to-law operator and attainable-set map are such that

P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} P_{\mathrm{base}}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\Phi(P_{\mathrm{MCD}}) \not\subseteq \operatorname{Down}(\Phi(P_{\mathrm{base}})).
\tag{A.7}

Proposition A.8. Under Assumption A.7, if P_{\mathrm{MCD}} \succeq_{\mathrm{MSD}} P_{\mathrm{base}}, then the induced attainable set of MCD is not contained in the downward closure of the baseline frontier.

Proof. Immediate from Assumption A.7. \square

Corollary A.9. H_{1b} (multivariate stochastic dominance) is a sufficient condition for H_{1a} (Pareto expansion) only under Assumption A.7. The converse does not hold.

Remark A.10. The scientific content resides in Assumption A.7 and in the operational definition (A.6). The question of when distributional dominance induces expansion of the attainable frontier remains an open mathematical problem. No claim is made that Assumption A.7 holds for arbitrary multivariate stochastic orders.

A.3. Center-Outward Distribution and Quantile Functions

The center-outward functions are defined via optimal transport, following Hallin et al. (2021).

Definition A.11 (Center-outward quantile function). For a Lebesgue-absolutely-continuous distribution P on \mathbb{R}^d with finite second moment, the center-outward quantile function is the optimal transport map from the uniform distribution U_d on \mathbb{B}_d to P:

Q_P^{\pm}: \mathbb{B}_d \to \mathbb{R}^d, \qquad Q_P^{\pm}{}_{\#}U_d = P.
\tag{A.8}

Definition A.12 (Center-outward distribution function). The center-outward distribution function is the inverse transport map:

F_P^{\pm}: \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{B}_d, \qquad F_P^{\pm}{}_{\#}P = U_d.
\tag{A.9}

The norm \|F_P^{\pm}(z)\| provides the center-outward rank of z; the direction F_P^{\pm}(z)/\|F_P^{\pm}(z)\| provides the sign.

Proposition A.13 (Regularity, conditional). Assume the regularity conditions required for the relevant center-outward optimal transport maps: absolute continuity of P with respect to Lebesgue measure, finite second moment, and nondegenerate support. Then the inverse relations

Q_P^{\pm} \circ F_P^{\pm} = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{R}^d}, \qquad F_P^{\pm} \circ Q_P^{\pm} = \mathrm{id}_{\mathbb{B}_d}
\tag{A.10}

hold P-almost everywhere and U_d-almost everywhere, respectively. Under stronger density and support conditions, the maps have the continuity and homeomorphism properties relevant to the empirical analysis; sufficient conditions are given in del Barrio, González-Sanz, and Hallin.

Proof. Follows from the standard theory of optimal transport for absolutely continuous measures with finite second moment, with regularity improvements under the additional density and support conditions. The relations are almost-everywhere, not global classical inverses; no claim of global homeomorphism is made without the stronger conditions. \square

Remark A.14 (Univariate reduction). In dimension d = 1, the center-outward distribution function reduces to the signed marginal CDF

F^{\pm}(x) = 2F(x) - 1.
\tag{A.11}

For d > 1, F^{\pm} is a genuinely multivariate transport map and is not the vector of marginal transforms.

A.4. Center-Outward q-Dominance

Definition A.15 (Quantile region). For a distribution P on \mathbb{R}^d and a quantile level q \in [0,1), the q-quantile region is

C_P(q) = Q_P^{\pm}(q \mathbb{B}_d) = \bigl\{ z \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|F_P^{\pm}(z)\| \leq q \bigr\}.
\tag{A.12}

Definition A.16 (q-dominance). For two distributions P and Q on \mathbb{R}^d and a quantile level q \in [0,1), P q-dominates Q, written P \succeq_q Q, if

\forall y \in C_Q(q), \qquad Q_P^{\pm}(F_Q^{\pm}(y)) \geq y,
\tag{A.13}

where the inequality is understood componentwise in the oriented coordinate system (A.4).

The restriction y \in C_Q(q) is essential: q-dominance is defined on the quantile region of the dominated distribution, not on the full support. The unqualified all-support relation does not constitute q-dominance.

Theorem A.17 (van der Laag et al., 2026). If P \succeq_q Q for all quantile levels q \in [0,1), then P strongly first-order stochastically dominates Q.

The protocol inherits this result by reference. The theoretical guarantee is not asserted independently.

A.5. Tolerance-Adjusted Violation Functional

The violation functional must correctly encode whether an MCD frontier point lies outside the tolerance-adjusted downward closure of the baseline frontier. The formulation requires care: the logical "for every q, at least one coordinate is violated" is not equivalent to any single max-of-infima expression.

Definition A.18 (Pairwise violation). For an MCD point p = (E_p, \mathcal{I}_p) and a baseline point q = (E_q, \mathcal{I}_q), the tolerance-adjusted violation of baseline q against MCD point p is

v(p, q)
=
\max
\left\{
\frac{E_p - E_q - \tau_E}{s_E},
\frac{\mathcal{I}_q - \mathcal{I}_p - \tau_{\mathcal{I}}}{s_{\mathcal{I}}}
\right\},
\tag{A.14}

with pre-registered tolerances \tau_E, \tau_{\mathcal{I}} and scale parameters s_E, s_{\mathcal{I}}.

Interpretation. Baseline q dominates MCD point p within tolerance iff

E_q \leq E_p + \tau_E
\quad\text{and}\quad
\mathcal{I}_q \geq \mathcal{I}_p - \tau_{\mathcal{I}},
\tag{A.15}

which is equivalent to v(p, q) \leq 0.

Definition A.19 (Point-level violation). The violation associated with an MCD point p relative to the baseline frontier \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}} is

\bar{v}(p)
=
\inf_{q \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}} v(p, q).
\tag{A.16}

Then \bar{v}(p) > 0 iff no baseline point dominates p within tolerance, i.e., p lies strictly outside the tolerance-adjusted downward closure of \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}.

Definition A.20 (Directional violation functional).

\Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}})
=
\sup_{p \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}} \bar{v}(p)
=
\sup_{p \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}}
\inf_{q \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}} v(p, q).
\tag{A.17}

Proposition A.21. \Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}) > 0 iff there exists an MCD point p \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}} such that no baseline point dominates p within tolerance.

Proof. If \Psi > 0, there exists p with \bar{v}(p) > 0, i.e., v(p, q) > 0 for all q \in \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}. By the interpretation (A.15), no baseline point dominates p within tolerance. Conversely, if such a p exists, then \bar{v}(p) > 0 and hence \Psi \geq \bar{v}(p) > 0. \square

Remark A.22. The characterization is clean because the point-level functional \bar{v}(p) is a single infimum, not a max-of-infima. The pairwise violation v(p, q) already contains the necessary logical information through its max structure. No additional logical gymnastics is required at the point level.

A.6. Null Hypothesis and Bootstrap Null Distribution for H1a

Definition A.23 (Null and alternative for H1a).

H_{1a,0}: \Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}^{\mathrm{pop}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}^{\mathrm{pop}}) \leq 0
\quad\text{(no tolerance-adjusted expansion)},
\tag{A.18}

H_{1a,1}: \Psi(\mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{MCD}}^{\mathrm{pop}}, \mathcal{F}_{\mathrm{base}}^{\mathrm{pop}}) > 0
\quad\text{(at least one MCD point lies outside the baseline downward closure)}.
\tag{A.19}

Designed null-generating bootstrap. A naive bootstrap under the alternative does not automatically produce a null distribution. The designed procedure generates a null-constrained bootstrap distribution by the following steps:

1. Estimate the empirical outcome distributions \hat{P}_{\mathrm{MCD}} and \hat{P}_{\mathrm{base}}.
2. Project the empirical MCD law onto the null region: define \tilde{P}_{\mathrm{MCD}} = \mathrm{Proj}_{H_{1a,0}}(\hat{P}_{\mathrm{MCD}}), where \mathrm{Proj}_{H_{1a,0}} is a pre-registered projection onto the set of outcome laws satisfying \Psi \leq 0 relative to \hat{P}_{\mathrm{base}}.
3. Resample SCM instances s from the observed set with replacement, retaining the lower-level hierarchy (d, m, i) within each resampled unit, and generate bootstrap outcomes from (\tilde{P}_{\mathrm{MCD}}, \hat{P}_{\mathrm{base}}).
4. Compute the bootstrap distribution of \hat{\Psi} under this null-generating scheme.

Rejection of H_{1a,0} at level \alpha would require the observed \hat{\Psi} to exceed the 1 - \alpha quantile of the null-generating bootstrap distribution.

Remark A.24. The projection \mathrm{Proj}_{H_{1a,0}} is a designed choice. It must be pre-registered and its properties (consistency, rate) analyzed before use. Alternative designs (subsampling calibration, confidence-set inversion) are admissible if pre-registered with equal rigor.

Remark A.25. The bootstrap resamples at the highest independent experimental unit (SCM instance), preserving the clustered design. No claim about generic mixing processes is made.

