Appendix B. Proper Scoring Rules and Utility Theory

B.1. Definition and Properties

Definition B.1 (Proper scoring rule). A scoring rule S(q, y) assigns a score to predictive distribution q and outcome y, with the convention that higher is better. The expected score under true distribution P is

S(q, P) = \mathbb{E}_{Y \sim P}[S(q, Y)].
\tag{B.1}

The rule is proper if

S(P, P) \geq S(q, P) \quad \forall q,
\tag{B.2}

and strictly proper if equality holds iff q = P almost everywhere.

Proposition B.2 (Log loss is strictly proper). The logarithmic scoring rule S_{\log}(q, y) = \log q(y) is strictly proper.

Proof. S_{\log}(q, P) - S_{\log}(P, P) = \mathbb{E}_P[\log(q/P)] = -\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}(P \| q) \leq 0, with equality iff q = P almost everywhere. \square

Definition B.3 (0–1 decision risk). For a predictive distribution q, the induced decision rule is

\delta_q = \arg\max_y q(y).
\tag{B.3}

The 0–1 risk of \delta_q under true distribution P is

R_{0\text{-}1}(\delta_q; P) = 1 - P(\delta_q).
\tag{B.4}

Proposition B.4. The 0–1 decision risk is minimized by any predictive distribution q whose induced decision rule \delta_q coincides with the Bayes-optimal class y^\star = \arg\max_y P(y). It does not uniquely identify the true distribution P.

Proof. Any q with \arg\max_y q(y) = y^\star induces the same decision \delta_q = y^\star, and hence the same risk 1 - P(y^\star). The degenerate distribution q = \delta_{y^\star} is a minimizer, yet q \neq P in general. \square

Corollary B.5. The baseline policy B4_{\text{Pred}} is designed to use a strictly proper scoring rule (e.g., log loss or Brier score) to avoid degenerate optima.

B.2. Equivalence of Log Loss and KL Divergence Under Matched Information

Proposition B.6. Let the interventional predictive adequacy score be

\mathcal{I}(\theta) = -\sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \, \mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl( P_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X) \,\|\, q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X) \bigr),
\tag{B.5}

and let the predictive utility of B4_{\text{Pred}} be

U(\theta) = \sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \, \mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}(X, Y)} \bigl[ \log q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X) \bigr].
\tag{B.6}

If both policies use the same interventional model q_\theta^{\mathit{do}}, then

\mathcal{I}(\theta) = U(\theta) - \sum_{e=1}^{E} w_e \, H_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}(Y \mid X),
\tag{B.7}

where H_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}(Y \mid X) = \mathbb{E}_{X \sim P_e^{\mathit{do}}}[H(P_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X))] is the conditional entropy of Y given X under the true interventional distribution in environment e.

Proof. The conditional KL divergence decomposes as

\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\bigl(P_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X) \| q^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)\bigr) = \mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}[\log P_e^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)] - \mathbb{E}_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}[\log q^{\mathit{do}}(Y \mid X)].
\tag{B.8}

The first term is the negative conditional entropy H_{P_e^{\mathit{do}}}(Y \mid X), independent of q^{\mathit{do}}. The second is the per-environment contribution to U(\theta). \square

Corollary B.7. Under identical predictive model classes, matched interventional information, and log loss, the MCD policy and B4_{\text{Pred}} optimize mathematically equivalent objectives up to a constant. At the objective level, the comparison contains no additional information-value term. Any observed difference, if the experiment were executed, would have to arise from differences in computation, approximation, optimization, search procedure, representation, or implementation.

Remark B.8. The qualification "under identical predictive model classes" is essential. Without it, the proposition does not apply: two policies using log loss on different function classes have different optimization landscapes.

B.3. Brier Score as an Alternative Proper Scoring Rule

Definition B.9 (Brier score). For categorical outcome y \in \{1, \ldots, K\} and predictive distribution q, the Brier score is

S_{\mathrm{Brier}}(q, y) = -\sum_{k=1}^{K} (q(k) - \mathbb{1}[y = k])^2.
\tag{B.9}

The sign is chosen so that higher is better, in agreement with the score convention.

Proposition B.10. The Brier score is strictly proper.

Proof. The expected Brier score under true distribution P is

S_{\mathrm{Brier}}(q, P) = -\sum_{k=1}^{K} (q(k) - P(k))^2 - \sum_{k=1}^{K} P(k)(1 - P(k)).
\tag{B.10}

The second term is independent of q. The first term is maximized uniquely at q = P. \square

Corollary B.11. If B4_{\text{Pred}} were to use the Brier score with the same interventional interface, the comparison would test whether the choice of proper scoring rule (KL vs. quadratic divergence) affects metareasoning performance. This is a scoring-rule robustness analysis, not an independent confirmation of the primary hypothesis.

B.4. Decision-Theoretic Foundations: One-Step and Continuation Values

Definition B.12 (One-step value of computation). The one-step value of computation of action a at history h_t is

\mathrm{VOC}_{\text{1-step}}(a \mid h_t)
=
\mathbb{E}\bigl[ U(\pi_{t+1}) - U(\pi_t) \mid h_t, a \bigr] - \lambda \cdot E(a).
\tag{B.11}

This quantity measures the immediate expected improvement from one action, net of its energy cost. It does not account for the information that the action may reveal about future opportunities.

Definition B.13 (Continuation value of computation). The continuation value of computation of action a at history h_t is

\mathrm{VOC}_{\text{cont}}(a \mid h_t)
=
\mathbb{E}\bigl[ V^\star(h_{t+1}) - V^\star(h_t) \mid h_t, a \bigr] - \lambda \cdot E(a),
\tag{B.12}

where V^\star(h) = \sup_\pi \mathbb{E}[\sum_{\tau \geq t} U(\pi_\tau) - \lambda E(a_\tau) \mid h_\tau = h] is the optimal continuation value.

Proposition B.14 (Optimal stopping with continuation VOC). The optimal stopping time is

\tau^\star = \inf\bigl\{ t : \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_t} \mathrm{VOC}_{\text{cont}}(a \mid h_t) \leq 0 \bigr\}.
\tag{B.13}

Proof. This follows from the dynamic programming principle. The value function satisfies the Bellman equation

V^\star(h_t) = \max\left\{ U(\pi_t), \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_t} \mathbb{E}[V^\star(h_{t+1}) \mid h_t, a] - \lambda E(a) \right\},
\tag{B.14}

and continuation is optimal iff the continuation value exceeds the stopping value. \square

Remark B.15. The one-step VOC and the continuation VOC coincide only when no future information value is generated by the current action. In general, \mathrm{VOC}_{\text{cont}} \geq \mathrm{VOC}_{\text{1-step}}. Any stopping rule based on \mathrm{VOC}_{\text{1-step}} alone is not guaranteed to be optimal for a sequential computation problem in which actions reveal information.

Proposition B.16 (Premature termination risk). Let \widehat{V}_t be any estimator of V_t satisfying the support condition

\mathbb{P}\bigl( \widehat{V}_t(a^\star) \leq \lambda E(a^\star) \bigr) > 0
\tag{B.15}

where a^\star = \arg\max_a [V_t(a) - \lambda E(a)] and V_t(a^\star) - \lambda E(a^\star) = \delta > 0. Then the naive stopping rule

\max_a \bigl[ \widehat{V}_t(a) - \lambda E(a) \bigr] \leq 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{terminate}
\tag{B.16}

terminates with positive probability at a step where the true optimal continuation value is strictly positive.

Proof. Under (B.15), the event \{ \widehat{V}_t(a^\star) \leq \lambda E(a^\star) \} has positive probability and implies termination under (B.16). \square

Remark B.17. The support condition (B.15) is a substantive assumption, not a consequence of positive variance. A random variable can have positive variance while being bounded above the threshold. The condition must be verified or assumed for the specific estimator.

Remark B.18 (Optimistic adjustment as design rule). The adjusted rule

\max_a \bigl[ \widehat{V}_t(a) + \beta_t s_t(a) - \lambda E(a) \bigr] \leq 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{terminate}
\tag{B.17}

uses a pre-registered optimism coefficient \beta_t and uncertainty estimate s_t(a). No confidence guarantee is claimed: \widetilde{V}_t is an optimistic value adjustment, not a statistically valid upper confidence bound. Its effect on premature-termination frequency is designed to be measured empirically in a pilot, if the pilot is executed.

