Appendix C. Information Geometry and Thermodynamic Preliminaries

C.1. Fisher–Rao Metric

Definition C.1. For a parametric family \mathcal{M} = \{p_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}, the Fisher information metric is

g_{ij}(\theta) = \mathbb{E}_{p_\theta}\!\left[ \partial_i \log p_\theta(X) \, \partial_j \log p_\theta(X) \right].
\tag{C.1}

Proposition C.2 (Invariance under reparameterization). The Fisher–Rao metric transforms as a (0,2)-tensor under smooth reparameterizations of \Theta.

Proof. Chain rule and substitution into (C.1) yield g_{ij}(\theta) = \sum_{k,l} \partial \phi^k/\partial \theta^i \, \partial \phi^l/\partial \theta^j \, g_{kl}(\phi). \square

C.2. Ruppeiner Metric and Thermodynamic Length

Definition C.3 (Ruppeiner metric). For a thermodynamic system with entropy S and extensive variables X = (X^1, \ldots, X^n),

g_{ij}^{R}(X) = -\frac{\partial^2 S}{\partial X^i \partial X^j}.
\tag{C.2}

Proposition C.4 (Relation to Fisher information). For suitable equilibrium statistical-mechanical families, the Ruppeiner metric can be related to the Fisher information metric, with the correspondence depending on the thermodynamic representation and assumptions.

Proof. Identifying extensive variables with the natural parameters of an exponential family, the entropy is the Legendre transform of the log-partition function, and its second derivatives coincide with the Fisher information in the natural parameterization. The correspondence is representation-dependent and requires the equilibrium assumption. \square

C.3. Thermodynamic Bounds for DNN Inference

Proposition C.5 (Tkachenko bound, model-dependent). Within the framework proposed by Tkachenko (2025), a model-dependent thermodynamic lower bound on inference energy for deep neural networks is derived from Landauer's principle applied to neuron state transitions. In this framework, linear operations can in principle be implemented reversibly, whereas nonlinear activation functions impose an irreducible energy cost determined by the average number of neurons undergoing state transition per inference.

Proof sketch. The derivation applies Landauer's principle to the erasure of information in neuron state transitions. Linear operations preserve information and can be implemented reversibly. Nonlinear activations discard information and require erasure at cost k_B T \ln 2 per bit. The bound is model-specific and depends on substrate class. \square

Remark C.6. The bound is not a general theorem about all DNN inference. It is a model-dependent result whose applicability depends on the substrate and the operational regime.

Corollary C.7. Any conjecture about a relation between measured material energy E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}} and thermodynamic entropy production \Sigma_{\text{prod}} must specify the class of substrate (dynamic vs. quasi-static analog, digital), because the bound differs qualitatively between these classes.

