Appendix D. Decision-Theoretic Framework

D.1. Primal Problem and Pareto Set

Definition D.1 (Policy space). Let \Pi be a set of computational policies, each mapping histories h_t to distributions over actions \mathcal{A}_t.

Definition D.2 (Operating point set). For a budget grid \mathcal{E} = \{E_1, \ldots, E_J\},

\mathcal{P} = \bigl\{ (E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi), \mathcal{I}(\pi)) : \pi \in \Pi \bigr\}.
\tag{D.1}

Proposition D.3 (Constrained optimum is nondominated). The constrained optimum

\pi^\star(E_{\max}) = \arg\max_{\pi \in \Pi} \bigl\{ \mathcal{I}(\pi) : E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi) \leq E_{\max} \bigr\}
\tag{D.2}

yields an operating point that is nondominated in \mathcal{P}.

Proof. Suppose p = (E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi^\star), \mathcal{I}(\pi^\star)) were dominated by q = (E_q, \mathcal{I}_q) \in \mathcal{P}. Then E_q \leq E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi^\star) \leq E_{\max} and \mathcal{I}_q \geq \mathcal{I}(\pi^\star). If either inequality is strict, this contradicts the optimality of \pi^\star in the constrained problem. \square

Remark D.4. The selected operating point is nondominated. This does not imply the policy \pi^\star is uniquely Pareto-optimal at the population level.

D.2. Penalty Parameter and Local Marginal Price

Definition D.5 (Lagrangian).

\mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) = \mathcal{I}(\pi) - \lambda \bigl( E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi) - E_{\max} \bigr).
\tag{D.3}

Definition D.6 (Penalty parameter). \lambda is an energy-performance penalty parameter, not a dual optimum in the convex-analysis sense.

Proposition D.7 (Local marginal price interpretation). When the empirical frontier is locally Lipschitz and admits a supporting hyperplane,

\frac{\partial}{\partial E_{\max}} \max_{\pi} \mathcal{I}(\pi) \approx \lambda^\star(E_{\max}).
\tag{D.4}

This is a local, empirical interpretation.

Proof. By the envelope theorem for the constrained problem, the derivative of the value function with respect to the constraint is the Lagrange multiplier, provided the constraint qualification holds locally. \square

Proposition D.8 (Non-convexity). The policy space \Pi is in general non-convex. The dual function g(\lambda) = \sup_\pi \mathcal{L}(\pi, \lambda) may not be differentiable, and primal recovery from the dual solution is not guaranteed.

Proof. Deterministic policies over a finite action space form a finite set, hence non-convex. Learned value functions introduce non-convexity through neural network composition. Primal recovery requires strong duality, which fails without convexity. \square

