Appendix E. Statistical Framework (Designed)

E.1. Hierarchical Model (Design)

The designed data-generating process is

\text{SCM instance} \;\to\; \text{dataset} \;\to\; \text{model seed} \;\to\; \text{inference seed}.
\tag{E.1}

Since MCD and baseline would be executed on the same SCM instances and datasets, the observations would be paired. The response variable is designed as

\Delta_{s,d,m,i} = Y_{s,d,m,i}^{\mathrm{MCD}} - Y_{s,d,m,i}^{\mathrm{base}},
\tag{E.2}

with the mixed model

\Delta_{s,d,m,i} = \mu + u_s + v_{s,d} + w_{s,d,m} + \varepsilon_{s,d,m,i},
\tag{E.3}

where

u_s \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_u^2), \quad v_{s,d} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_v^2), \quad w_{s,d,m} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_w^2), \quad \varepsilon_{s,d,m,i} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_\varepsilon^2).
\tag{E.4}

Indices: s = 1, \ldots, S (SCM instance), d = 1, \ldots, D (dataset), m = 1, \ldots, M (model seed), i = 1, \ldots, I (inference seed).

E.2. Variance Components and ICC

Definition E.1 (Intra-class correlations). With total variance

\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2 = \sigma_u^2 + \sigma_v^2 + \sigma_w^2 + \sigma_\varepsilon^2,
\tag{E.5}

the variance fractions are

\rho_u = \frac{\sigma_u^2}{\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2}, \quad \rho_v = \frac{\sigma_v^2}{\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2}, \quad \rho_w = \frac{\sigma_w^2}{\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2}, \quad \rho_\varepsilon = \frac{\sigma_\varepsilon^2}{\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2}.
\tag{E.6}

Proposition E.2 (Variance of the grand mean). Under the hierarchical model (E.3),

\operatorname{Var}(\bar{\Delta})
= \frac{\sigma_u^2}{S} + \frac{\sigma_v^2}{SD} + \frac{\sigma_w^2}{SDM} + \frac{\sigma_\varepsilon^2}{SDMI}.
\tag{E.7}

Equivalently, in terms of the variance fractions,

\operatorname{Var}(\bar{\Delta})
= \frac{\sigma_{\text{tot}}^2}{S D M I} \bigl( \rho_u D M I + \rho_v M I + \rho_w I + \rho_\varepsilon \bigr).
\tag{E.8}

Proof. The variance of the sum of independent random effects is the sum of their variances. Dividing by the square of the total number of observations SDMI for the grand mean, and using \sigma_k^2 = \rho_k \sigma_{\text{tot}}^2, yields (E.8). \square

E.3. Simulation-Based Power (Designed)

Definition E.3 (Simulation-based power). For candidate N_{\text{SCM}}, the designed simulation-based power estimate is

\widehat{\mathrm{Power}}_{H_{1a}}(N) = \frac{\#\{r : H_{1a}^{(r)} \text{ satisfied}\}}{R},
\tag{E.9}

where each replication r is designed to simulate the complete experiment: SCM instances, datasets, model seeds, inference seeds, operating points, frontier construction, and evaluation of H_{1a}.

Proposition E.4 (Consistency). As R \to \infty, \widehat{\mathrm{Power}}_{H_{1a}}(N) \to \mathrm{Power}_{H_{1a}}(N) almost surely.

Proof. Strong law of large numbers. \square

E.4. H1b Power as a Separate Quantity

Definition E.5 (H1b power). The designed H_{1b} power is estimated under a separately specified data-generating scenario:

\mathrm{Power}_{H_{1b}}(N) = \mathbb{P}\bigl( \text{\(H_{1b}\) correctly detected at } N \bigr).
\tag{E.10}

Joint power. The joint quantity

\mathrm{Power}_{\mathrm{joint}}(N) = \mathbb{P}\bigl( H_{1a} \text{ detected} \wedge H_{1b} \text{ detected at } N \bigr)
\tag{E.11}

is designed to be reported as an additional diagnostic. H_{1b} power is not silently conditioned on H_{1a} being satisfied: the estimand is defined independently, and the joint power is reported separately.

E.5. Pilot-Based Calibration of N_{\text{SCM}} (Design)

The pilot is designed to output the posterior median \tilde{\rho}_u. The designed mapping from \tilde{\rho}_u to N_{\text{SCM}} is determined by simulation:

\rho_u \;\longrightarrow\; \widehat{\mathrm{Power}}_{H_{1a}}(N; \rho_u) \;\longrightarrow\; N^\star(\rho_u) = \min\{N : \widehat{\mathrm{Power}}_{H_{1a}}(N; \rho_u) \geq 0.80\}.
\tag{E.12}

The pre-registered grid \{20, 30, 45\} is designed as the discretization of N^\star(\rho_u) over the pre-registered prior range \rho_u \in [0, 0.50]. Values of \tilde{\rho}_u > 0.50 are pre-registered to trigger protocol suspension.

E.6. Sequential Gated Analysis (Design)

Definition E.6 (Gated analysis). The designed procedure is:

1. Test the null hypothesis H_{1a,0} of no tolerance-adjusted expansion at level \alpha_1.
2. If H_{1a,0} is rejected (i.e., the primary expansion claim is supported), proceed to test the null hypothesis H_{1b,0} of no q-dominance at level \alpha_2.
3. H_{1b} is treated as a secondary gated analysis and is only formally tested after the preregistered H_{1a} criterion is met.

Remark E.7 (Terminology). The phrase "reject H_{1a,0}" is used consistently. The phrase "H_{1a} is rejected" is avoided, since it would invert the intended logic and could be misread as rejection of the primary claim.

Under the global null and the Bonferroni-style level allocation, the family-wise error rate for the pair (H_{1a,0}, H_{1b,0}) would be bounded by \alpha_1 + \alpha_2. This is a conservative union bound; no general theorem about nested hypotheses is invoked.

E.8. Replication Criterion Across Families (Design)

The primary hypothesis H_{1a} is designed to be tested per family F_1, F_2, F_3. The pre-registered decision rule is a replication criterion:

\sum_{f=1}^{3} \mathbb{1}\{H_{1a,0} \text{ rejected in } F_f\} \geq 2.
\tag{E.13}

This is a distinct construct from a conventional global hypothesis test. It asks whether the phenomenon would survive across distinct SCM families, not whether a single pooled null would be rejected. The 2-of-3 rule does not carry a conventional global Type I error interpretation and is not reported as one.

E.9. Multiple Testing Across Families

The secondary hypotheses H_2 are designed to be tested across families with Holm correction. The primary hypothesis H_{1a} is reported per family and assessed by the replication criterion (E.13).

E.10. Cross-Family Coefficient of Variation (Design)

\mathrm{CV}_j = \frac{\operatorname{SD}_{i \in \{1,2,3\}}[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)]}{\operatorname{Mean}_{i \in \{1,2,3\}}[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)]}, \qquad j = 1, \ldots, 5.
\tag{E.14}

Criterion: \max_j \mathrm{CV}_j \leq 0.20, with auxiliary condition \operatorname{Mean}_i[\lambda^\star(F_i, E_j)] > \epsilon_\lambda = 10^{-3}. This is designed to measure cross-family variation only. It does not measure intra-family stability, between-seed stability, between-budget stability, or optimizer stability. The auxiliary condition ensures numerical stability of the ratio, but it does not by itself guarantee interpretability if \lambda^\star can approach zero or become negative. If \lambda^\star is conceptually nonnegative by construction, this should be stated explicitly; otherwise an alternative dispersion statistic (e.g., normalized MAD) is pre-registered.

E.11. Dual Ascent Convergence Diagnostic (Design)

Definition E.8 (Budget discrepancy).

\Delta_{\text{dual}}(F_i, E_j) = \bigl| \mathbb{E}[E_{\mathcal{B}_{\text{hw}}}(\pi^\star(\lambda^\star_{k_{\max}}))] - E_{\max} \bigr|.
\tag{E.15}

Definition E.9 (Budget noise scale). The scale \sigma_{E,\mathrm{budget}} is designed to be estimated from repeated measurements of the entire primitive/policy run, propagating the power-measurement uncertainty into integrated energy. It is not the instantaneous power sampling noise.

Criterion. If \Delta_{\text{dual}}(F_i, E_j) > \eta \cdot \sigma_{E,\mathrm{budget}} with \eta = 3 on any (F_i, E_j), the corresponding \lambda^\star is pre-registered to be flagged as non-converged and excluded from the CV computation.

E.12. Bayesian ICC Estimation in the Pilot (Design)

The pilot is designed to use Bayesian estimation of the variance components with weakly informative prior

\rho_u \sim \text{Beta}(1.5, 3).
\tag{E.16}

The posterior median \tilde{\rho}_u and 95% credible interval are designed to be reported.

E.13. Program-Level Falsification Criterion (Design)

The program separates three levels of evidence:

Primary program-level falsification. The primary MCD claim would be falsified at the program level if H_{1a,0} fails to be rejected on at least two of three families.

Secondary evidence. H_2 fails across families.

Strong distributional claim. H_{1b} fails under the van der Laag framework.

Definition E.10 (Program-level falsification).

\text{Primary MCD claim falsified} \iff \#\{f : H_{1a,0} \text{ not rejected in } F_f\} \geq 2.
\tag{E.17}

No post-hoc loosening of exclusion criteria. No post-hoc selection of the winning budget. No modification of hypotheses after observation. H_2 and H_{1b} are reported separately and not combined into a composite p-value.

Remark E.11. The term "program-level falsification" is used deliberately to distinguish this criterion from a statistical hypothesis test. The MCD program is a research program, not a single statistical hypothesis; its rejection at the program level is a decision about whether to continue the research line, not a probabilistic inference about a parameter.

