Appendix F. Leakage Audit (Designed)

F.1. Forbidden Set

Definition F.1 (Forbidden set).

\mathcal{F}_{\text{forbidden}}
=
\bigl\{
Y_{\text{test}},\;
\mathcal{I}^\star,\;
\text{future outcomes},\;
\text{unreleased labels},\;
\text{unexposed SCM state}
\bigr\}.
\tag{F.1}

Remark F.2 (Causal interface distinction). SCM ground truth available through the authorized interventional interface is not forbidden: the protocol is designed to supply interventional semantics by construction, as stated in the main text. The distinction

\text{interventional semantics supplied} \neq \text{causal model identified}

governs the leakage definition. Only quantities that the policy is not authorized to observe are forbidden.

F.2. Static Provenance Audit

Definition F.3 (Provenance graph). G = (V, E) has vertices V = h_t \cup \mathcal{F}_{\text{forbidden}} \cup \{\text{policy output}\} and edges representing data dependencies in the computation.

Proposition F.4 (Graph-relative static certification). Within the declared provenance graph G, absence of a directed path from any v \in \mathcal{F}_{\text{forbidden}} to the policy output is sufficient for static non-leakage certification relative to G.

Proof. A directed path from a forbidden variable to the policy output in G constitutes a data dependency recorded in G, which is the definition of leakage relative to G. \square

Remark F.5 (Scope of certification). The certification is relative to the declared provenance graph. It does not establish absence of leakage in the real execution unless the graph is complete with respect to the actual computational dependencies. Undeclared dependencies are not covered by the certificate. This limitation is analogous to the probe insufficiency result (Proposition F.9) and is essential to preserve.

F.3. Canary Audit

Definition F.6 (Canary variable). A canary variable z_{\text{canary}} is a synthetic variable designed to be injected into a channel that should not affect the policy. It is correlated with \mathcal{I}^\star with strength \rho_{\text{canary}}.

Definition F.7 (Canary leakage statistic). The empirical mutual information between policy decisions and the canary is \widehat{I}(\pi; z_{\text{canary}}).

Inferential calibration (design). The empirical mutual information is designed to be compared against a permutation null distribution constructed by randomly permuting canary assignments across experimental units. The canary statistic is a diagnostic; the threshold \epsilon_{\text{canary}} is a pre-registered diagnostic parameter, not a population-level certificate.

F.4. Predictive Probe Diagnostic

Definition F.8 (Probe). A probe f_{\text{probe}} is a function class designed to predict \mathcal{I}^\star from h_t. The probe R^2 is the fraction of variance explained.

Definition F.9 (Multi-probe diagnostic). Multiple probes \mathcal{G}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{G}_K of increasing capacity are designed. Leakage is pre-registered to be flagged if any probe exceeds the pre-registered diagnostic threshold, calibrated on a pilot run with known absence of leakage.

Proposition F.10 (Probe insufficiency). No finite probe can certify the absence of leakage.

Proof. Leakage is defined by the existence of a measurable function of h_t that predicts forbidden information. A probe tests one function class. If the probe fails, leakage may still exist via a function outside the probe's hypothesis class. The set of measurable functions is uncountable. \square

Remark F.11. Failure to detect leakage is a diagnostic, not a proof of absence.

