Appendix G. Manifest Locking (Designed Mechanism)

Definition G.1 (Manifest).

\mathcal{M} = ( \text{metric}, \text{comparator}, \text{threshold}, \text{dataset}, \text{seed}, \text{pipeline\_hash}, \text{scm\_generator\_hash}, \text{policy\_code\_hash} ).
\tag{G.1}

Definition G.2 (Canonical encoding). The canonical encoding \text{canonical}(\mathcal{M}) is a deterministic, byte-exact serialization of the manifest fields under a fixed schema. Hash stability requires identical encoding rules across runs.

Definition G.3 (Manifest hash).

H(\mathcal{M}) = \text{SHA-256}(\text{canonical}(\mathcal{M})).
\tag{G.2}

Proposition G.4 (Tamper evidence, second-preimage assumption). Assume the standard second-preimage resistance of SHA-256. Then, for a fixed locked manifest \mathcal{M} with hash H(\mathcal{M}), finding an altered manifest \mathcal{M}' \neq \mathcal{M} with H(\mathcal{M}') = H(\mathcal{M}) is computationally infeasible under current cryptographic assumptions.

Proof. Standard second-preimage resistance hypothesis. \square

Remark G.5 (Terminology). The property established is tamper evidence, not tamper detection: a matching hash establishes consistency with the locked digest, but does not prove that the original configuration was truthful. Only the combination of (i) the locked hash, (ii) the recomputed hash, (iii) deterministic canonicalization, and (iv) identical manifest encoding supports the verdict machinery.

Designed verification procedure. If an execution were performed, the observed configuration would be canonicalized and hashed. Verification would return:

· PASS if the recomputed hash equals the locked hash and the observed configuration matches.
· FAIL if the recomputed hash equals the locked hash but the observed results do not meet the preregistered criteria.
· TAMPERED if the recomputed hash differs from the locked hash.

