Appendix J. Baseline Comparison Redesign (Theoretical)

J.1. Algebraic Equivalence Under Matched Information

Proposition J.1. Under identical predictive model classes, matched interventional information, and log loss, the MCD policy and B4_{\text{Pred}} optimize objectives equivalent up to a constant independent of \theta. At the objective level, the comparison contains no additional information-value term; any observed difference, if the experiment were executed, would have to arise from differences in computation, approximation, optimization, search procedure, representation, or implementation.

Proof. See Proposition B.6 and Corollary B.7. \square

J.2. Primary Comparison: Value of Causal Information

Option A (Value of Causal Information). MCD is designed to have access to the interventional interface (x, m, x_{\mathit{do}}). The baseline B4_{\text{Pred}} is designed to use only observational data (x, y). The comparison is designed to measure the value of interventional semantics for computational allocation.

H_2^{\mathrm{VoCI}}: \Delta \mathcal{I}(E_3) > 0 \text{ vs } B4_{\text{Pred}} \text{ (observational)}.
\tag{J.1}

Central identification issue. The observed difference

\Delta_{\text{observed}}(E)
=
\mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{MCD}}(E) - \mathcal{I}_{\text{observational}}(E)
\tag{J.2}

decomposes, in general, as

\Delta_{\text{observed}}
=
\Delta_{\text{information}}
+
\Delta_{\text{model}}
+
\Delta_{\text{optimization}}
+
\Delta_{\text{implementation}}
+
\varepsilon.
\tag{J.3}

The causal attribution target is \Delta_{\text{information}}, not merely the total observed difference. Identification of \Delta_{\text{information}} requires that the following dimensions be experimentally controlled as far as possible:

· model family;
· parameter count;
· training budget;
· inference budget;
· action space;
· stopping rule;
· scoring rule;
· implementation stack.

Without such control, \Delta_{\text{observed}} confounds the information effect with model, optimization, and implementation effects. The confounding structure is not a nuisance; it is part of the estimand definition.

J.3. Secondary: Scoring-Rule Robustness Analysis

Option B. Both policies would use the interventional interface, but MCD would optimize KL divergence while B4_{\text{Pred}} would optimize the Brier score.

H_2^{\mathrm{Brier}}: \Delta \mathcal{I}(E_3) > 0 \text{ vs } B4_{\text{Pred}} \text{ (Brier)}.
\tag{J.4}

This is a scoring-rule robustness analysis, not an independent confirmation of the primary hypothesis.

J.4. Tertiary: Observational Invariance-Based Baseline

Option C. B4_{\text{Pred}} would use invariance-based prediction on observational data, with the same action space and energy measurement. The baseline is described as observational invariance-based, with documented theoretical and empirical limitations as reported in the literature.

J.5. Power Analysis for the Redesigned Comparison (Design)

For candidate N_{\text{SCM}}, the design simulates the complete experiment with both policies and estimates

\widehat{\mathrm{Power}}_{\mathrm{VoCI}}(N) = \frac{\#\{\text{simulated experiments satisfying } H_2^{\mathrm{VoCI}}\}}{R}.
\tag{J.5}

The smallest N giving at least 0.80 power under a preregistered effect-size scenario is pre-registered as the target.

