Appendix K. Independence of q-Dominance Frameworks

K.1. Primary and Robustness Frameworks

Primary: van der Laag et al. (2026) for H_{1b}. Robustness: Ma, Liu, and Zhuang (2025) as an independent analysis.

K.2. Non-Interchangeability

The two frameworks define different notions of multivariate stochastic dominance:

· van der Laag et al.: q-dominance based on the optimal transport map between the unit ball and the target distribution; empirical test uses center-outward grids, ranks, and signs.
· Ma et al.: multidimensional stochastic dominance based on contribution functions over center-outward contours and regions, with Kolmogorov–Smirnov and Cramér–von Mises statistics and bootstrap validity.

Proposition K.1. The frameworks are not interchangeable. A result under one framework does not automatically transfer to the other.

Proof. The MSD definitions differ in their treatment of the quantile level and the transport map geometry. The empirical statistics differ in construction and asymptotic behavior. Bootstrap procedures are validated under different regularity conditions. \square

K.3. Protocol Requirement (Design)

No mixing of definitions, interpretations, or bootstrap procedures across frameworks. The primary framework is designed for H_{1b}. The robustness framework is designed only to check whether the qualitative conclusion would be stable across frameworks, if the experiment were executed.

