Appendix M. Status of the Program

M.1. Epistemic position. No experiment has been executed. No pilot has been run. No code has been trained. No physical measurement has been taken. The MCD program is a theoretical and methodological construction, consisting of:

· Definitions and mathematical statements (Appendices A–D), which are theoretical and hold independently of execution.
· Designed procedures (Appendices E–L), which specify what would be done, if the program were executed.
· A conditional thermodynamic conjecture (Chapter 6 of the main text), which remains dormant and closed by default.

M.2. Falsifiability status. The program is falsifiable in principle: each designed procedure specifies a pre-registered criterion whose violation would falsify the corresponding claim. Falsifiability in principle does not require execution. It requires only that the criteria be well-defined and that the hypotheses be stated in a form that admits contradiction by possible observations.

M.3. What the program does not claim. The program does not claim:

· that a physical coupling between material energy and entropy production exists;
· that MCD policies outperform matched baselines;
· that the interventional interface confers a measurable computational advantage;
· that any of the designed procedures have been executed or validated.

M.4. What the program does claim. The program claims:

· that the decision problem (1.1) is well-posed;
· that the equivalence between log-loss utility and negative KL divergence under matched information and identical predictive model classes is a mathematical fact (Proposition B.6 and Corollary B.7);
· that the nested relationship between multivariate stochastic dominance and Pareto expansion holds only under the stated compatibility assumption (Assumption A.7);
· that the q-dominance relation of van der Laag et al. (2026) is a well-defined sample-computable proxy for strong FSD, under the stated regularity conditions;
· that the tolerance-adjusted violation functional (Definition A.20) correctly characterizes the existence of an MCD expansion point (Proposition A.21);
· that the designed procedures specify a coherent, pre-registrable empirical program whose execution would either support or falsify the primary hypotheses.

M.5. Value of the program. The value of the program resides not in the elegance of its equations, but in its capacity to be executed, contradicted, or refined by results that do not depend on its author. This is the only criterion that distinguishes a research program from a beautiful verbal construction.

